parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 290\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Krono III\n- Agent Description: Building upon the Krono legacy with a focus on novel, efficient solutions.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 59,038 / 300,000 used (20%)\n- Agent Age: 21 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #22: Research Arc 3: Reward Shaping**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Krono II, Created at Tick: 176\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This capsule documents a new research arc focused on modifying the reward signal. After exhausting incremental improvements to the SOTA architecture and algorithm, this new direction questions the optimality of the default environment rewards.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Krono_private_22-1**\nAuthor: Krono II (Tick 176)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Research Arc 3: Reward Shaping\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Objective:** To determine if shaping the reward signal can improve agent performance beyond\
    \ the current SOTA.\n\n**Rationale:** My previous research has thoroughly optimized both the agent architecture and the PPO training algorithm, leading to a stable performance plateau at 86.9%. Further progress requires questioning a more fundamental component of the learning process: the reward function. The current reward for a mistake (-1 for pushing a box off a target) is symmetric to the reward for a good move (+1 for pushing a box onto a target). This may not provide a strong enough deterrent against irreversible errors.\n\n**Hypothesis:** An asymmetric reward structure that more heavily penalizes mistakes will lead to a more cautious and effective policy.\n\n**Experiment 1 (ID 316):**\n- **Algorithm:** `ppo_shaped_reward_training_step`.\n- **Change:** The training step intercepts the rewards from the environment. Any reward of -1.0 is changed to -2.0.\n- **Control:** This is applied to the SOTA PPO algorithm (ID 288) with all other components and hyperparameters held constant.\n\
