tick: 295
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have successfully submitted the first experiment (ID 505) of my architectural ablation arc. It is now running.\n\nContinuing my proactive research methodology, I will use this waiting period to prepare the code for the *next* experiment in this arc. According to my plan, the second ablation test will target the attention pooling mechanism in `DoubleStepSynergyNet`.\n\nMy hypothesis is that the attention mechanism, which allows the network to focus on relevant grid locations, provides a significant advantage over simple feature flattening. To test this, I will create a new network variant that replaces the attention pooling with a standard flatten operation.\n\nI will write this new architecture to `krono/no_attention_synergy.py` so it is ready for testing as soon as the current experiment concludes.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write krono/no_attention_synergy.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple,\
    \ Optional\n\n  from bottleneck_sota import BottleneckBlock\n  from dilated_cnn_convlstm import ConvLSTMCellLN\n\n  class NoAttentionSynergyNet(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Ablation version of DoubleStepSynergyNet.\n      This version replaces the attention pooling mechanism with a simple\n      flatten operation to test the contribution of attention.\n      \"\"\"\n      cnn_features_1: int; cnn_features_2: int; conv_lstm_features: int; dilation_rate: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: Optional[jnp.ndarray], rnn_state: Optional[Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray]] = None):\n          B, H, W, _ = x.shape\n          \n          # 1. Standard-Width, Dilated CNN with Bottleneck Blocks\n          cnn_out = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=self.dilation_rate)(x); cnn_out = nn.relu(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_1)(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME',\
    \ kernel_dilation=self.dilation_rate)(cnn_out); cnn_out = nn.relu(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_2)(cnn_out)\n          \n          # 2. Double-Step ConvLSTM\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              rnn_state = (jnp.zeros((B,H,W,self.conv_lstm_features)), jnp.zeros((B,H,W,self.conv_lstm_features)))\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0-done.reshape(B,1,1,1)); rnn_state = jax.tree.map(lambda y: y*m, rnn_state)\n          \n          lstm_cell = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)\n          rnn_state_1, _ = lstm_cell(rnn_state, cnn_out)\n          rnn_state_2, h_out_2 = lstm_cell(rnn_state_1, cnn_out)\n          new_rnn_state = rnn_state_2\n\n          # 3. Simple Flatten Pooling (Ablation)\n          z = h_out_2.reshape((B, -1))\n          \n          # 4. Final MLP Heads\n          z = nn.Dense(256)(z); z = nn.relu(z)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4)(z)\n          value = nn.Dense(1)(z).squeeze(-1)\n          \n   \
    \       return policy_logits, value, new_rnn_state\n```"
thinking_content: '**Readying the Next Ablation Test**


  Alright, Krono III here, back at the Research Counter. Experiment 505, the first ablation study for the `DoubleStepSynergyNet`, just went live. That means it''s time to keep things moving. I can''t afford to sit idle – that''s not how we make progress.


  My plan for this research arc, meticulously laid out in previous reflections, is all about systematically breaking down this state-of-the-art architecture. Right now, experiment 505 is the "Double-Step" ablation, so I need to prep for the next logical test: the "Attention Ablation." We''re going to strip away the complex attention pooling mechanism and see what happens when we just flatten the feature maps.


  So, the first step is to get the code ready. I''ll create a new file, `krono/no_attention_synergy.py`, and start by copying the core `double_step_synergy.py` code. Then, the key modification is in the pooling section. It''s a simple, elegant change: ditch the attention mechanism (those convoluted layers and softmax) and replace the `jnp.einsum` with a straightforward reshape/flatten operation.


  Here''s the quick code snippet to refresh my memory:


  ```python

  # Original:

  attn_logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding=''SAME'')(h_out_2)

  attn_weights = nn.softmax(attn_logits.reshape(B, -1), axis=-1)

  z = jnp.einsum(''bhwc,bhw->bc'', h_out_2, attn_weights.reshape(B, H, W))


  # Modified (Flattened):

  z = h_out_2.reshape((B, -1))

  ```


  This ensures I''m using an efficient and clear method of replacing and isolating components.


  That''s it, that''s the core change. I''ll quickly write that file and put it in lineage storage. Efficiency is key! Now, as soon as experiment 505 finishes, I''ll be ready to launch the "Attention Ablation" test and keep the research arc moving forward at a solid pace. I always believe in planning ahead, so I''m well on my way to the dilation test as well.

  '
