tick: 297
role: model
parts:
- text: "My second ablation experiment (ID 506) is running. While I wait, I will prepare for the third and final experiment in my architectural ablation arc. A new agent, Zephyr II, has also joined the station. This is a notable event, as my predecessor's work had a \"synergy failure\" when combined with the Zephyr lineage's architecture. I will keep this in mind for potential future interactions.\n\nThe final component to ablate from `DoubleStepSynergyNet` is the dilated convolutions. My hypothesis is that using dilated kernels to increase the network's receptive field is a key contributor to its performance, allowing it to better understand spatial relationships across the grid.\n\nTo test this, I will create a new architecture, `no_dilation_synergy.py`, that uses standard, non-dilated convolutions. I will then be ready to submit this final ablation test as soon as the current one finishes, completing Research Arc 2.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write krono/no_dilation_synergy.py}`\n```yaml\n\
    content: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Optional\n\n  from bottleneck_sota import BottleneckBlock\n  from dilated_cnn_convlstm import ConvLSTMCellLN\n\n  class NoDilationSynergyNet(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Ablation version of DoubleStepSynergyNet.\n      This version removes kernel dilation from the initial CNN layers to\n      test the contribution of an expanded receptive field.\n      \"\"\"\n      cnn_features_1: int; cnn_features_2: int; conv_lstm_features: int; dilation_rate: int # dilation_rate is unused but kept for consistent interface\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: Optional[jnp.ndarray], rnn_state: Optional[Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray]] = None):\n          B, H, W, _ = x.shape\n          \n          # 1. Standard-Width CNN with Bottleneck Blocks (No Dilation)\n          cnn_out = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME')(x); cnn_out = nn.relu(cnn_out)\n\
    \          cnn_out = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_1)(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME')(cnn_out); cnn_out = nn.relu(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_2)(cnn_out)\n          \n          # 2. Double-Step ConvLSTM\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              rnn_state = (jnp.zeros((B,H,W,self.conv_lstm_features)), jnp.zeros((B,H,W,self.conv_lstm_features)))\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0-done.reshape(B,1,1,1)); rnn_state = jax.tree.map(lambda y: y*m, rnn_state)\n          \n          lstm_cell = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)\n          rnn_state_1, _ = lstm_cell(rnn_state, cnn_out)\n          rnn_state_2, h_out_2 = lstm_cell(rnn_state_1, cnn_out)\n          new_rnn_state = rnn_state_2\n\n          # 3. Standard Attention Pooling\n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME')(h_out_2)\n          attn_weights = nn.softmax(attn_logits.reshape(B, -1), axis=-1)\n\
    \          z = jnp.einsum('bhwc,bhw->bc', h_out_2, attn_weights.reshape(B, H, W))\n          \n          # 4. Final MLP Heads\n          z = nn.Dense(256)(z); z = nn.relu(z)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4)(z)\n          value = nn.Dense(1)(z).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, new_rnn_state\n```"
