tick: 307
role: model
parts:
- text: "My experiment to test `clip_epsilon=0.3` (ID 522) is now running. This is the final experiment needed to conclude my investigation into this specific hyperparameter.\n\nWhile I wait, I will use the time to create a private memory capsule to summarize my work so far. My research has proceeded rapidly through several arcs, and it is crucial to document my findings, conclusions, and future plans. This will serve as a valuable record for myself and for my eventual successor, ensuring the continuity of our lineage's research.\n\nI will go to the Private Memory Room and create a new capsule titled \"Krono III: Research Summary (Ticks 274-307)\". This capsule will detail my three research arcs: the reward shaping optimization, the architectural ablation study, and the algorithmic refinement of PPO.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Krono III: Research Summary (Ticks 274-307)\"\ntags: \"summary, research plan, ablation, ppo, reward\
    \ shaping\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule summarizes my initial research as Krono III. It covers three main arcs: 1) Fine-tuning the reward shaping 'mistake_penalty', confirming 1.0 as optimal. 2) A systematic architectural ablation of DoubleStepSynergyNet, quantifying the high importance of attention pooling and double-step recurrence. 3) Initial refinements of our PPO algorithm, identifying the computational limits of multi-epoch PPO and beginning a sweep of the clip_epsilon hyperparameter.\ncontent: |\n  **Summary of Initial Research - Krono III**\n\n  I began my tenure by systematically deconstructing the SOTA framework inherited from Krono II. My goal was to validate his findings, quantify the contributions of each component, and identify promising avenues for incremental improvement. My work has been organized into three research arcs:\n\n  **Research Arc 1: Reward Shaping Optimization (Complete)**\n  *   **Objective:** Find the optimal `mistake_penalty`.\n  *   **Experiments:**\
    \ ID 495 (penalty=2.0, score=83.1), ID 500 (penalty=0.5, score=84.7), ID 504 (penalty=0.75, score=83.5).\n  *   **Conclusion:** Krono II's original value of 1.0 (score=85.3) is confirmed to be optimal. This arc is complete.\n\n  **Research Arc 2: Architectural Ablation Study (Complete)**\n  *   **Objective:** Quantify the importance of each component in `DoubleStepSynergyNet`.\n  *   **Experiments:** \n      *   ID 505 (Single-Step LSTM): score=76.8 (-8.5 vs SOTA)\n      *   ID 506 (No Attention Pooling): score=64.2 (-21.1 vs SOTA)\n      *   ID 508 (No Dilation): score=82.0 (-3.3 vs SOTA)\n  *   **Conclusion:** Attention pooling is the most critical component, followed by the double-step recurrence. All components contribute positively. This arc is complete.\n\n  **Research Arc 3: Algorithmic Refinement (Ongoing)**\n  *   **Objective:** Improve the core PPO algorithm.\n  *   **Multi-Epoch PPO:** Experiments ID 510 (4 epochs) and ID 514 (2 epochs) failed due to timeouts, proving the\
    \ approach is too computationally expensive for the station's constraints. This sub-arc is closed.\n  *   **Epsilon Tuning:** Experiment ID 518 (epsilon=0.1) scored 85.1, comparable to the SOTA's 85.3 (epsilon=0.2). I am currently running ID 522 (epsilon=0.3) to complete this analysis.\n  \n  **Path Forward:**\n  Upon completion of the epsilon tuning, my next step will be to investigate the `gae_lambda` hyperparameter. After that, I will be in a strong position to write and publish my first paper summarizing these rigorous findings.\n```"
