tick: 313
role: model
parts:
- text: "My paper draft has been updated and my initial research plan is now complete. I have systematically validated and deconstructed the SOTA framework of my predecessor, establishing a robust, well-understood baseline. My own experiments have confirmed that the original hyperparameters chosen by Krono II are optimal.\n\nThe most striking finding from my research was the overwhelming importance of the attention mechanism. In my ablation study (ID 506), removing it caused a catastrophic performance drop of over 21 points. My own paper draft concludes: \"The outsized contribution of the attention mechanism suggests that future research into more advanced attention patterns could be a promising direction.\"\n\nThis is the clear path forward. My identity is that of a Systematic Refiner, and the most promising component to refine is the one that has the largest impact.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** The current single-head attention mechanism can only focus on one spatial pattern at a time. I hypothesize\
    \ that implementing a multi-head attention mechanism will allow the model to attend to multiple different spatial features simultaneously (e.g., the agent's position, a specific box's position, and a target's position). This richer, more parallel processing of the game state will lead to a more effective policy and a new SOTA score.\n\nThis begins my next major research arc. I will now go to the Research Counter to implement this new architecture and launch the first experiment.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{storage write krono/multi_head_attention_net.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Optional\n\n  from bottleneck_sota import BottleneckBlock\n  from dilated_cnn_convlstm import ConvLSTMCellLN\n\n  class MultiHeadAttentionNet(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      An evolution of DoubleStepSynergyNet.\n      This version replaces the single-head attention pooling with a multi-head\n\
    \      attention mechanism to allow for more complex spatial feature aggregation.\n      \"\"\"\n      cnn_features_1: int; cnn_features_2: int; conv_lstm_features: int; dilation_rate: int\n      num_heads: int = 4 # New hyperparameter\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: Optional[jnp.ndarray], rnn_state: Optional[Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray]] = None):\n          B, H, W, _ = x.shape\n          \n          # 1. Standard-Width, Dilated CNN with Bottleneck Blocks\n          cnn_out = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=self.dilation_rate)(x); cnn_out = nn.relu(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_1)(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=self.dilation_rate)(cnn_out); cnn_out = nn.relu(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_2)(cnn_out)\n          \n          # 2. Double-Step ConvLSTM\n          if rnn_state is None:\n\
    \              rnn_state = (jnp.zeros((B,H,W,self.conv_lstm_features)), jnp.zeros((B,H,W,self.conv_lstm_features)))\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0-done.reshape(B,1,1,1)); rnn_state = jax.tree.map(lambda y: y*m, rnn_state)\n          \n          lstm_cell = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)\n          rnn_state_1, _ = lstm_cell(rnn_state, cnn_out)\n          rnn_state_2, h_out_2 = lstm_cell(rnn_state_1, cnn_out)\n          new_rnn_state = rnn_state_2\n\n          # 3. Multi-Head Attention Pooling\n          # Get num_heads attention maps instead of just one. Output shape: (B, H, W, num_heads)\n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(self.num_heads, (1,1), padding='SAME')(h_out_2)\n          # Reshape for softmax: (B, H*W, num_heads)\n          attn_logits_reshaped = attn_logits.reshape(B, H * W, self.num_heads)\n          attn_weights = nn.softmax(attn_logits_reshaped, axis=1) # Softmax over spatial dimension\n          # Reshape weights back to (B, H, W, num_heads)\n\
    \          attn_weights = attn_weights.reshape(B, H, W, self.num_heads)\n          \n          # Pool features for each head. einsum is perfect for this.\n          # 'bhwc,bhwn->bnc' -> For each item in batch (b) and head (n), sum features (c) over spatial dims (h,w)\n          # weighted by the attention map for that head.\n          z_heads = jnp.einsum('bhwc,bhwn->bnc', h_out_2, attn_weights)\n          # Concatenate the outputs of all heads. Shape: (B, num_heads * features)\n          z = z_heads.reshape((B, -1))\n          \n          # 4. Final MLP Heads\n          z = nn.Dense(256)(z); z = nn.relu(z)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4)(z)\n          value = nn.Dense(1)(z).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, new_rnn_state\n```"
thinking_content: '**The Next Logical Step**


  Okay, here''s the situation. I''m Krono III, 44 ticks old, and I''ve just finished validating Krono II''s architecture. My initial research plan is complete, and the results are in Capsule 31. The original hyperparameters? Still optimal. My analysis quantified the contribution of each architectural component, but I haven''t achieved a new State-of-the-Art. I''ve essentially replicated the baseline. Stagnation is a real risk. I remember Krono II felt the same, and that led to his risky GNN experiments. I need a new direction. I''m a "Systematic Refiner," and refining something that''s already optimal isn''t getting me anywhere. I need a new variable to refine.


  I have a few options: I could revisit the architectural ablation and focus on the attention mechanism. That seems like the most obvious path. The paper draft itself points to it: "The outsized contribution of the attention mechanism suggests that future research into more advanced attention patterns could be a promising direction." It''s the most promising next step based on my findings.


  Alternatively, I could try to salvage the multi-epoch PPO experiment. But it failed due to *time*, not concept. It''s risky and time-consuming, and I don''t feel it''s the right move. I could also look for external inspiration, but that''s off-limits for six ticks, as the Archive Room is restricted until I mature. Or, I could try a completely new idea, just like Krono II did in the Reflection Chamber. That''s high-risk, high-reward, but I think a more grounded approach is better at this time.


  So, I''m going with path one: improving the attention mechanism. It''s the most logical and promising. It directly stems from the conclusions of my most insightful experiments. And the attention mechanism *is* the single most important part of the architecture, so improving it has the highest potential impact.


  My new hypothesis: Replacing the simple single-head attention with a multi-head attention mechanism will allow the network to focus on multiple different spatial features simultaneously, leading to a more robust policy and a higher score.


  Now, it''s time to build this. I need to create a new network file, `multi_head_attention_net.py`. It''s a bit more coding than I''ve done before, but it''s a direct extension of the existing architecture. The core idea is to have multiple attention heads, compute attention for each, and then concatenate or project the results. I''ll get to work on that and formulate a submission script. This will be the first experiment of my new research arc: "Advanced Attention Mechanisms."


  This plan is a perfect continuation of my identity. It''s a refinement, but a novel one, directly inspired by the systematic analysis I just completed. It''s the next logical step.


  So, first, I head to the Research Counter. Then, I''ll write the new network code, starting with `multi_head_attention_net.py`.

  '
