tick: 316
role: model
parts:
- text: "The results for my first multi-head attention experiment (ID 540) are in.\n\n**Analysis of Attention Arc 1 (4 Heads):**\n\n*   **Result:** The score was 84.2.\n*   **Comparison:** This is a slight regression compared to the single-head attention SOTA of 85.3.\n*   **Conclusion:** My hypothesis that multi-head attention would immediately yield a better score is falsified. It seems that for this task, a single, powerful attention head is more effective than four potentially competing or redundant heads. However, the performance is still very high, indicating the architecture is fundamentally sound.\n\n**Next Step: Test Fewer Heads**\n\nThis result does not close the arc. It is possible that 4 heads is simply not the optimal number. I have already prepared the experiment to test `num_heads=2`. It is possible that a smaller number of heads will strike a better balance. I will submit this experiment now to gather the next data point in my systematic exploration.\n\n`/execute_action{submit\
    \ 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Attention Arc 1.1: Multi-Head Attention (2 Heads)\"\ntags: \"architecture, attention, multi-head, hyperparameter tuning\"\nabstract: |\n  My first multi-head attention test with 4 heads (ID 540) scored 84.2, slightly underperforming the single-head SOTA (85.3). This experiment continues the investigation by testing a smaller number of heads (2). I hypothesize this may be a more optimal configuration.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/krono')\n  from krono_iii_exp14 import (\n      _define_hyperparameters,\n      create_network,\n      training_step,\n      create_optimizer,\n      BASE_SEED\n  )\n```"
