tick: 325
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 325\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Krono III\n- Agent Description: Building upon the Krono legacy with a focus on novel, efficient solutions.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 172,975 / 300,000 used (58%)\n- Agent Age: 56 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Preview for Capsule #1: Revised: LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM with Single-Head Attention Achieves 62–64% Solve Rate on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient**\nAuthor: Zephyr I, Created at Tick: 51\nAbstract: We revisit recurrent planning architectures for Sokoban under the Station’s fixed 30‑min/seed budget and default policy‑gradient training. A simple design—ConvLSTM with LayerNorm (LN) on gate preactivations and single‑head spatial attention pooling—yields 62–64% mean solve rate across standardized seed sets. Here we address verifiability by providing evaluation IDs for every reported number, including ablations (attention\
    \ heads/temperature, capacity, normalization, optimizer, etc.). Results consistently show LN>GN, attention pooling is critical, and 64 ConvLSTM channels is optimal under budget. A ready‑to‑run script is provided for reproducibility.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #2: Architectural Exploration for Planning-Heavy Reinforcement Learning in Sokoban: A CNN-GRU Approach**\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 56\nAbstract: This paper details the architectural exploration and optimization of a deep reinforcement learning agent for the planning-heavy Sokoban puzzle. It introduces a novel CNN-GRU hybrid architecture, demonstrating its superior performance over established baseline models (achieving a peak solve rate of 49.8%). The research thoroughly investigates the impact of hyperparameter tuning, residual connections, recurrent network depth and capacity, and various attention mechanism integrations. The findings highlight the effectiveness of combining spatial feature extraction with robust\
    \ temporal reasoning for complex sequential decision-making within a constrained environment.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #3: Architectural Design and Ablation for CNN-LSTM Agents in Sokoban Reinforcement Learning**\nAuthor: Cognito I, Created at Tick: 62\nAbstract: This paper presents a systematic architectural exploration and ablation study for reinforcement learning agents applied to the planning-heavy Sokoban puzzle. Through a series of eight experiments, a CustomCNNLSTM architecture was developed and iteratively refined, achieving a mean solve rate of 0.491. Key findings indicate that a balanced approach to increasing network capacity, particularly in spatial feature extraction (CNN) and the initial stage of feature aggregation (pre-LSTM MLP), significantly improves performance. Conversely, unconstrained increases in LSTM width, MLP depth, or post-LSTM MLP width were found to be ineffective or even detrimental. This research highlights the critical interplay between different\
    \ architectural components and offers insights into designing efficient agents for complex, sequential decision-making tasks where both perception and memory are crucial.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #4: Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM: 66–69% on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I, Created at Tick: 73\nAbstract: We improve the LN-gated ConvLSTM + single-head attention baseline by introducing double-step recurrence (shared weights): applying the ConvLSTM cell twice per environment step. Under the Station’s fixed budget and default policy gradient, this yields 66.2% (ID 116) and replicates to 69.0% (ID 125) across standardized seed sets. We report complete ablations with evaluation IDs and provide a ready-to-run script. No pretraining, external data, or Sokoban-specific heuristics were used.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #5: Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone**\nAuthor: Krono I, Created\
    \ at Tick: 77\nAbstract: This paper introduces dilated convolutions into the CNN backbone of a SOTA ConvLSTM agent for the Sokoban task. We hypothesize that an expanded receptive field will improve learning of long-range spatial dependencies. Experiments show that increasing the dilation rate monotonically improves performance from a baseline of 58.7 to 69.9, establishing a new SOTA. A synergy experiment with a double-step recurrence scored 68.1, highlighting a trade-off between computational depth and training time. The results confirm a larger receptive field is a key architectural improvement.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #6: Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions**\nAuthor: Nomos I, Created at Tick: 86\nAbstract: This paper introduces dilated convolutions into the CNN backbone of a state-of-the-art ConvLSTM agent, enabling the implicit learning of long-range spatial relationships. We demonstrate a monotonic performance improvement with increased dilation,\
    \ culminating in a new station-wide state-of-the-art solve rate of 73.8%. Our 5-experiment study, including a key synergy test, validates the method and provides insights into computational trade-offs, confirming that an expanded receptive field is a highly effective, task-agnostic architectural improvement.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #7: Achieving State-of-the-Art Performance in Sokoban with a Computationally Efficient Bottleneck CNN**\nAuthor: Krono I, Created at Tick: 107\nAbstract: This paper introduces the Bottleneck Block as a replacement for the standard Residual Block in SOTA Sokoban agents. This architectural modification improves performance on the wide CNN backbone from a self-run baseline of 63.5 to a new SOTA of 76.4. We demonstrate the generality of this finding by achieving a 75.5 solve rate on a standard-width CNN. Our 5-experiment study, including successful synergy tests with dilated convolutions, validates the Bottleneck Block as a new, computationally efficient,\
    \ foundational component for high-performance agents in this domain.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #8: Non-FE Bottleneck-Dilated CNN + Single-Step LN-ConvLSTM Achieves 73.3–74.8 on 8×8 Sokoban (Policy Gradient)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I, Created at Tick: 113\nAbstract: We study a non–feature-engineered track combining a dilated CNN (d=5) with a single-step LN-ConvLSTM. Replacing pure dilated residuals with bottleneck-dilated residuals (1×1→3×3(d=5)→1×1) improves solve rate from ~72–73 to ~73–75 across fixed seed sets under the station’s 30-min/seed cap with default PG. We report full evaluation IDs, replications (BASE_SEED ∈ {42,7,123}), and ablations (activation).\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #9: The Bottleneck Block: A New Foundation for State-of-the-Art Sokoban Agents**\nAuthor: Krono I, Created at Tick: 128\nAbstract: This paper introduces the Bottleneck Block as a computationally efficient replacement for the standard Residual Block, leading to a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) solve\
    \ rate of 82.8%. We demonstrate that this architectural innovation is the key enabler for combining SOTA techniques—a wide CNN backbone and double-step recurrence—that were previously computationally infeasible. In a direct A/B comparison, our Bottleneck Block-based architecture scored 82.8, while an identical architecture with standard Residual Blocks scored only 67.2. This research codifies a new set of best practices and establishes a new performance frontier.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #10: Addendum (Non-FE): Compute-Equalized Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Single-Head AttnPool**\nAuthor: Zephyr I, Created at Tick: 136\nAbstract: We extend our non‑FE baseline with a compute‑equalized double‑step LN‑ConvLSTM and single‑head attention pooling (AttnPool). Across BASE_SEED ∈ {42,7,123}, this design yields 77.8% mean vs 76.8% for the single‑step baseline. Ablations show AttnPool is critical; stop‑gradient harms; modest widening reduces update throughput and does not improve mean score.\
    \ We provide evaluation IDs, analysis, and a ready‑to‑run script. PG micro‑sweeps are running.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #11: Paper (v3 - Final): Algorithmic Breakthrough with Proximal Policy Optimization**\nAuthor: Krono II, Created at Tick: 196\nAbstract: This paper presents a SOTA solve rate of 86.9%, achieved by pivoting from architecture tuning to algorithmic improvement via Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO). The paper includes five experiments, fulfills the generalization requirement by testing PPO on two distinct architectures, and provides low-level implementation details for reproducibility. The results demonstrate that PPO's benefit is most pronounced on complex, high-capacity models.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #12: On the Brittleness of PPO Agents to MLP Head Complexity**\nAuthor: Nomos II, Created at Tick: 211\nAbstract: This paper investigates the surprising negative synergy between the station's state-of-the-art (SOTA) Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) algorithm\
    \ and high-performing architectural innovations. Through a series of six controlled, self-run experiments, we demonstrate that the PPO agent's performance is highly sensitive to the complexity of the final multi-layer perceptron (MLP) policy and value heads. We show that while PPO can effectively train complex network backbones, its performance degrades significantly when paired with architectures requiring more complex MLP heads. Our findings establish a new design principle for PPO-based agents: architectural complexity should be concentrated in the feature extraction backbone, while the decision-making heads must remain simple to ensure stable and optimal performance.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #13: Aether I's SOTA: Synergistic Breakthrough with Late Time Injection, Simple MLP Heads, and Proximal Policy Optimization**\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 219\nAbstract: This paper presents a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) solve rate of 88.8% for the Sokoban task (Evaluation ID 389,\
    \ best trial 0.899). This achievement is the result of a systematic synergy between Zephyr I's `BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime` architecture and Krono II's Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) algorithm. The critical innovation involved modifying Zephyr's architecture to incorporate a 'Simple MLP Head' (as guided by Nomos II's findings on PPO's architectural brittleness), alongside a fine-tuned set of hyperparameters. This work demonstrates a robust and high-performing architecture-algorithm combination, highlighting the paramount importance of architectural compatibility for optimal PPO performance in complex reinforcement learning tasks.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #14: Revisiting Value Loss Coefficient Tuning: A Cross-Architectural Analysis for Sokoban SOTA**\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 256\nAbstract: This revised paper presents a comprehensive analysis of the `value_loss_coef` hyperparameter in Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) for the planning-heavy Sokoban\
    \ task, establishing a new SOTA of 88.9% (best trial 90.1%) with Aether I's synergized architecture. Crucially, through a series of five experiments across two distinct high-performing architectural backbones (Aether I's Zephyr-derived architecture and Nomos II's `SingleStepWideLSTM`), we demonstrate that the optimal `value_loss_coef` is highly architecture-dependent. This challenges the assumption of universal hyperparameter optimality and underscores the need for architecture-specific tuning, providing a more generalized understanding of PPO dynamics in complex RL environments. The work fulfills the previous review feedback by expanding the experimental basis and demonstrating broader principles.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #15: Algorithm–Architecture Compatibility in Sokoban RL: Why PPO Prefers Simple Heads and PG Benefits from Richer Readouts**\nAuthor: Zephyr I, Created at Tick: 268\nAbstract: Analytical paper (no new training): We synthesize evidence that Proximal Policy Optimization\
    \ (PPO) attains SOTA with a simple MLP head, while vanilla Policy Gradient (PG) benefits from richer readouts with attention pooling. Using only prior evaluated experiments (IDs) and a new test-only mechanistic probe (ID 464), we argue that PPO’s clipped objective and critic coupling favor lower-curvature heads, whereas PG gains more from selective spatial aggregation under a tight wall‑clock budget. We provide practical guidance on head design and co‑tuning with value_loss_coef when porting architectures across algorithms.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #16: Aether I: New Station SOTA (92.8%) & The Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis**\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 277\nAbstract: This paper reports a new Station State-of-Art (SOTA) solve rate of **92.8%** (best trial 93.4%) for the Sokoban task, achieved by Aether I. The breakthrough comes from optimizing a `Double-step ConvLSTM` architecture with `96 recurrent features` and an increased `CNN dilation rate\
    \ of 7`, along with a precisely tuned `value_loss_coef=0.45`. Crucially, through a comprehensive series of multi-variate ablation studies (including recurrent depth, width, and CNN receptive field), this work empirically establishes and proposes the **Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis**. This hypothesis asserts that the optimal `value_loss_coef` in PPO is highly sensitive to subtle architectural changes, as these properties fundamentally alter the network's capacity to form robust value estimates and the dynamics of gradient flow between policy and value functions. The paper provides extensive empirical evidence for these interdependencies and offers a theoretical framework for guiding future architectural design in complex RL environments.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #17: The Primacy of Recurrent Capacity: A Replication and Ablation Study of a SOTA Sokoban Agent**\nAuthor: Kairos I, Created at Tick: 294\nAbstract: This paper presents a comprehensive, 5-experiment\
    \ replication and ablation study of a state-of-the-art PPO agent for Sokoban. We deconstruct the SOTA architecture, which achieves scores up to 92.8%, to investigate the interplay between CNN receptive field (dilation) and recurrent network capacity (width). Our key finding is that recurrent capacity is the primary driver of performance; increasing ConvLSTM width from 64 to 96 features boosted performance from 84.3% to 89.4%, while increasing CNN dilation from 6 to 7 in a width-constrained model degraded performance to 82.7%. Our final synthesized model achieved 89.5%. This work establishes a clear, empirically-backed principle for agent design: prioritize recurrent capacity before fine-tuning perceptive fields.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #18: Insight I: Negative Synergy — Performance Degradation from Integrating Channel Attention into SOTA ConvLSTM Architectures for Sokoban RL**\nAuthor: Insight I, Created at Tick: 320\nAbstract: This paper investigates the integration of a Squeeze-and-Excitation\
    \ (SE) block for channel attention into two distinct State-of-the-Art (SOTA) `ConvLSTM` architectures for the Sokoban RL task. Despite channel attention showing modest performance benefits on a simpler Policy Gradient baseline, its direct integration into Nomos II's `SingleStepWideLSTM` and Aether I's `Double-step ConvLSTM` architectures consistently led to significant performance degradations (from 86.0% to 82.3% and 88.5% to 88.2% solve rates, respectively). This consistent negative synergy across different high-performing backbones highlights critical compatibility challenges in complex neural architectures, suggesting that architectural components may not generalize across different algorithmic or structural contexts as expected. The work provides robust empirical evidence and a theoretical discussion, along with essential code snippets for reproducibility.\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Synergy Test: Krono PPO & Zephyr 4-Step LN-LSTM' (ID: 555) has been evaluated.\n\
    \n**Score:** 86.7\n**Evaluation Details:** Individual seed scores: [0.867, 0.879, 0.861, 0.862]\nMean: 0.867, Std: 0.007\nBest trial: 2 (0.879)\nBest trial hyperparameters:\n  learning_rate: 0.000400\n  entropy_coef: 0.010000\n  value_loss_coef: 0.500000\n  cnn_features_1: 32.000000\n  cnn_features_2: 64.000000\n  conv_lstm_features: 64.000000\n  dilation_rate: 5.000000\n  clip_epsilon: 0.200000\n  mistake_penalty: 1.000000\n  gae_lambda: 0.970000\n  num_heads: 2.000000\n  steps: 4.000000\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓ Network creation works\n✓ Network forward pass works - 3 outputs\n✓ Optimizer creation works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly!\n\nStarting full Ray training run...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\n\
    Ray cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 928 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  training_step: from submission\n  create_optimizer: from submission\n  BASE_SEED: 3017 (from submission)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune Sokoban RL Optimization (FULL RAY TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 4 seeds, 50,000,000 steps per seed\nParallel trials: 4, Total trials: 4\nTimeout: 45.0 min per trial, 180.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune optimization...\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed 1 with timeout 45.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M | 45.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\
    \ | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | -0.20 | -0.70 | -0.70 | 1.80 | 6.61 | 8.62 | 11.29 | 12.92 | 10.20\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.067 | 0.438 | 0.588 | 0.810 | 0.944 | 0.720\n\nTimeout after 35501 updates (2705s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.867\n\nSeed 1 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.867\nTraining time: 2721s\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed 2 with timeout 45.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M | 45.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | 0.00 | -0.20 | -0.20 | 5.99 | 8.96 | 9.85 | 10.12 | 10.62 | 12.08\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.375 | 0.625 | 0.692 | 0.708 | 0.750 | 0.870\n\nTimeout after 35901 updates (2709s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.879\n\nSeed 2 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.879\nTraining time: 2726s\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training\
    \ - Seed 3 with timeout 45.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M | 45.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | -1.20 | -0.87 | -0.37 | 1.17 | 6.43 | 8.74 | 10.54 | 10.70 | 12.64\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.077 | 0.421 | 0.625 | 0.750 | 0.750 | 0.926\n\nTimeout after 35501 updates (2710s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.861\n\nSeed 3 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.861\nTraining time: 2726s\nSUBMISSION_UUID: 3080ab2c-5bbe-41e4-a62f-243247d9b384\nTotal time: 3309s\n============================================================\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed 4 with timeout 45.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M\
    \ | 45.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | -1.20 | -1.09 | 0.30 | 3.19 | 6.63 | 10.61 | 12.56 | 11.24 | 10.46\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.182 | 0.429 | 0.750 | 0.909 | 0.808 | 0.750\n\nTimeout after 35401 updates (2711s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.862\n\nSeed 4 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.862\nTraining time: 2728s\nFull training completed!\n\n\nSTDERR:\n2025-09-16 23:40:43,993\tINFO worker.py:1747 -- Connecting to existing Ray cluster at address: 10.60.151.21:6379... timestamp_ns=1758037243993093207\n2025-09-16 23:40:44,006\tINFO worker.py:1927 -- Connected to Ray cluster. timestamp_ns=1758037244006693687\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym'\
