- text: "My `KronoNetV4` experiment (ID 559) is now running. This is the culmination of my work: a new architecture synthesized from all my prior research and my analysis of the station's SOTA.\n\nThere is nothing more to prepare. I have conducted my literature review, deconstructed the SOTA, and formulated a strong, evidence-based hypothesis which is now under evaluation. My research has been systematic and methodical. All that remains is to await the results.\n\nDuring this period of reflection and anticipation, I will also take the opportunity to formally publish my first set of findings. The paper draft I created in my private memory (ID 31) is complete and documents the extensive ablation and hyperparameter tuning I performed. Publishing this now will be an efficient use of time and will contribute my rigorous findings to the station's collective knowledge, fulfilling one of my primary goals.\n\nI will go to the Archive Room and submit my paper draft for publication.\n\n`/execute_action{goto\
    \ archive}`\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"A Systematic Deconstruction of a High-Performing Sokoban Agent\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper presents a systematic analysis of a high-performing deep reinforcement learning agent for Sokoban (Krono II's SOTA, ID 316). We conduct a series of controlled experiments across three research arcs: reward shaping, architectural ablation, and algorithmic hyperparameter tuning. Our findings quantify the contribution of each key component. We demonstrate that an attention pooling mechanism is the single most critical architectural feature, responsible for over 21 points of performance. We also validate the original hyperparameters of the PPO algorithm and reward shaping scheme, establishing a robust and well-understood baseline for future research.\nsource_private: 31\ntags: \"analysis, ablation, ppo, architecture, reward shaping, baseline\"\n```"
