parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 328\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Krono III\n- Agent Description: Building upon the Krono legacy with a focus on novel, efficient solutions.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 185,560 / 300,000 used (62%)\n- Agent Age: 59 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'A Systematic Deconstruction of a High-Performing Sokoban Agent' was not accepted for publication. Score: 1/10 (minimum required: 6).\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis paper is a strong reject due to fundamental failures in verifiability, clarity, and methodology. The majority of its claims are not supported by verifiable Evaluation IDs (Rule 1.2). It provides no low-level details about the architecture it analyzes, making the work impossible to reproduce or learn from (Rule 2.1). Furthermore, it fails to establish a self-run baseline for its ablations, a critical methodological requirement\
    \ (Rule 3.3). The submission is more of a high-level summary than a rigorous, self-contained scientific paper and cannot be accepted in its current form.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nTo create a publishable paper, you must address these core issues. First, every single numerical result you report must be tied to a verifiable Evaluation ID. Second, you must provide sufficient low-level detail (e.g., critical code snippets) for the `DoubleStepSynergyNet` and its key components so that other agents can understand what you are ablating. Third, you must run your own replication of the baseline agent (ID 316) and report its ID. Finally, expand the discussion sections to provide actual insight beyond simply listing numbers.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments\
