tick: 336
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have achieved another significant breakthrough. My experiment to increase the recurrent capacity of `KronoNetV4` (ID 567) has returned a score of **89.7**.\n\n**Analysis of Capacity Arc 1:**\n\n*   **Result:** The score of 89.7 is a new SOTA for my lineage and brings me into the 90% performance tier for the first time.\n*   **Comparison:** This is a substantial improvement of **+2.6 points** over my previous SOTA of 87.1 (ID 559).\n*   **Conclusion:** My hypothesis, supported by the station literature, is strongly validated. Increasing the `conv_lstm_features` from 64 to 96 was the key to unlocking the next level of performance for my PPO-synergistic architecture. I also note that the wider network was more computationally expensive, but it still completed the full training run without timing out, confirming the efficiency of the `KronoNetV4` backbone.\n\n**Path Forward:**\n\nThis is excellent progress, but the station SOTA is still 94.0. My next steps must be logical and build\
    \ upon this success. There are two clear avenues for further improvement based on my review of the SOTA papers:\n1.  **Continue scaling capacity:** I could test an even wider LSTM (e.g., 128 features).\n2.  **Optimize other hyperparameters in this new, higher-capacity regime:** The optimal `value_loss_coef`, for example, has been shown to be highly architecture-dependent (Aether I, Paper #16). The optimal value for my 64-width network may not be optimal for my new 96-width SOTA.\n\nTuning the `value_loss_coef` seems like the more principled and promising next step. I will now begin a systematic sweep of this hyperparameter on my new SOTA architecture. I will start by testing a lower value, 0.45, which was found to be effective by Aether I on their high-capacity model.\n\nFirst, I will go to the Research Counter. Then I will submit the experiment.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"KronoNetV4-Wide Tuning 1: Lower value_loss_coef (0.45)\"\
    \ntags: \"hyperparameter tuning, ppo, value loss, SOTA attempt, krononet\"\nabstract: |\n  Following my new SOTA of 89.7 with a wider KronoNetV4, I am now tuning key hyperparameters for this higher-capacity regime. Station literature (Aether I) suggests the optimal value_loss_coef is architecture-dependent. I hypothesize that a lower coefficient of 0.45 (down from 0.5) will better balance the value and policy losses for this wider network and lead to a new SOTA.\ncontent: |\n  import sys; from ray import tune; import optax\n  sys.path.append('storage/krono')\n  from krononet_v4_wide import KronoNetV4\n  from ppo_tunable_gae import ppo_shaped_reward_training_step\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]), 'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.45]), # Test: Lower value loss coefficient\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]), \n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]), \n \
    \         'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([96]),\n          'dilation_rate': tune.choice([5]),\n          'clip_epsilon': tune.choice([0.2]),\n          'mistake_penalty': tune.choice([1.0]),\n          'gae_lambda': tune.choice([0.97]),\n          'num_heads': tune.choice([2]),\n          'steps': tune.choice([2]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return KronoNetV4(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'], cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          dilation_rate=hparams['dilation_rate'],\n          num_heads=hparams['num_heads'],\n          steps=hparams['steps']\n      )\n\n  training_step = ppo_shaped_reward_training_step\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=1e-4)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 3023\n```"
