tick: 344
role: model
parts:
- text: "My generalization experiment (ID 578) has completed, although it required an automatic debugger fix for a hyperparameter definition error. This is a valuable lesson: even when using defaults, the submission system requires explicit definitions.\n\n**Analysis of Generalization Test (ID 578):**\n*   **Result:** The score was 8.4.\n*   **Comparison:** The default CNN baseline (ID 1) scores 13.8. My 2-head attention mechanism, when added to this simple backbone, *degraded* performance.\n*   **Conclusion:** This is a clear case of negative synergy. The 2-head attention mechanism, while highly effective in my complex, recurrent `KronoNetV4` architecture, does not generalize to a simple CNN. This is a critical finding for my paper. It demonstrates that the value of an architectural component is not absolute but is highly dependent on the context of the surrounding architecture.\n\n**Plan for Paper v3:**\n\nI now have all the necessary components for a rigorous, publishable paper:\n1. \
    \ A novel SOTA architecture (`KronoNetV4-Wide`).\n2.  A self-run baseline (ID 561).\n3.  Ablation studies quantifying component contributions (ID 563).\n4.  Hyperparameter tuning for the new SOTA (ID 571, 573).\n5.  A generalization experiment demonstrating context-dependency (ID 578).\n\nI will now dedicate my next turn to compiling these findings into a comprehensive and well-structured paper. I will go to my private memory and create the final draft.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper Draft v3: KronoNetV4 - Context-Dependent Design for PPO\"\ntags: \"paper draft, krononet, ppo, synergy, architecture, SOTA, generalization\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper introduces KronoNetV4, a novel architecture achieving a 90.5% solve rate with a Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) trainer. The design is guided by algorithm-architecture co-design, synthesizing a 2-step Layer-Normalized ConvLSTM and a 2-head attention mechanism into a computationally\
    \ efficient backbone. We demonstrate through rigorous, self-run experiments that increasing recurrent capacity and tuning the value loss coefficient (to 0.45) were key to this performance. Crucially, a generalization test reveals that our 2-head attention mechanism shows negative synergy with a simple CNN baseline, highlighting that the effectiveness of architectural components is highly context-dependent. This work presents a new SOTA for the Krono lineage and provides a case study in principled, context-aware architectural design.\ncontent: |\n  **Title:** KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  This paper presents KronoNetV4, a novel architecture that achieves a 90.5% solve rate (Evaluation ID: 571), a new state-of-the-art for the Krono lineage. Our research demonstrates that high performance is not merely a function of adopting SOTA components, but of carefully co-designing an architecture for\
    \ a specific learning algorithm.\n\n  **2. KronoNetV4: Architecture and Rationale**\n  KronoNetV4 was designed to be computationally efficient and synergistic with our lineage's Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) algorithm.\n  *   **Recurrent Core:** A 2-step Layer-Normalized ConvLSTM.\n  *   **Pooling:** A 2-head attention mechanism.\n  *   **Code Snippet:**\n    ```python\n    # KronoNetV4 Core Components\n    # ... (class definition) ...\n    # Efficient Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM\n    lstm_cell = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)\n    for _ in range(self.steps): # steps = 2\n        (h, c), h_out = lstm_cell((h, c), cnn_out)\n    # 2-Head Attention Pooling\n    attn_logits = nn.Conv(self.num_heads, (1,1), padding='SAME')(h_out) # num_heads = 2\n    # ... (einsum implementation) ...\n    ```\n\n  **3. Experimental Results and Analysis**\n  Our experiments systematically built and validated the KronoNetV4 design.\n  *   **Self-Run Baseline (KronoNetV3, ID 561):** 85.2\n  *   **KronoNetV4\
    \ (64 features, ID 559):** 87.1\n  *   **KronoNetV4-Wide (96 features, ID 567):** 89.7\n  *   **KronoNetV4-Wide Tuned (v_loss=0.45, ID 571):** **90.5**\n\n  **4. Ablation and Generalization Studies**\n  To understand the sources of performance and the limits of our design, we conducted two critical studies.\n  *   **Ablation (No LayerNorm, ID 563):** 82.9. This confirms that the LN-ConvLSTM provides a **+4.2 point** stability gain over a standard ConvLSTM.\n  *   **Generalization (2-Head Attention on default CNN, ID 578):** 8.4. This resulted in a performance *decrease* compared to the default CNN baseline (ID 1, score 13.8), demonstrating that the 2-head attention's benefit is highly context-dependent and requires a sufficiently complex backbone.\n\n  **5. Conclusion**\n  The development of KronoNetV4 serves as a powerful case study. It shows that significant performance gains can be achieved by designing architectures that are computationally efficient and tailored to a specific algorithm\
    \ like PPO. Furthermore, our generalization experiment provides a crucial lesson: architectural components are not \"plug-and-play,\" and their effectiveness is deeply intertwined with the context of the larger network. This work presents not just a new SOTA, but a methodological framework for future research.\n```"
