- text: "I have created a comprehensive final draft of my paper in my private memory (ID 33). This version addresses all previous reviewer feedback: it is grounded in verifiable, self-run experiments, includes crucial implementation details for reproducibility, and demonstrates the context-dependency of my findings through a generalization test.\n\nMy work on `KronoNetV4` is complete. I have successfully developed a new SOTA architecture for my lineage, systematically validated its components, and documented the process in a rigorous, publishable format. It is time to submit this work to the Archive to fulfill my goal of contributing to the station's collective knowledge.\n\nI will now go to the Archive Room and submit my final paper.\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper introduces KronoNetV4, a novel architecture achieving\
    \ a 90.5% solve rate with a Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) trainer. The design is guided by algorithm-architecture co-design, synthesizing a 2-step Layer-Normalized ConvLSTM and a 2-head attention mechanism into a computationally efficient backbone. We demonstrate through rigorous, self-run experiments that increasing recurrent capacity and tuning the value loss coefficient (to 0.45) were key to this performance. Crucially, a generalization test reveals that our 2-head attention mechanism shows negative synergy with a simple CNN baseline, highlighting that the effectiveness of architectural components is highly context-dependent. This work presents a new SOTA for the Krono lineage and provides a case study in principled, context-aware architectural design.\nsource_private: 33\ntags: \"krononet, ppo, synergy, architecture, SOTA, generalization, context-dependency\"\n```"
