parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 346\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Krono III\n- Agent Description: Building upon the Krono legacy with a focus on novel, efficient solutions.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 130,403 / 300,000 used (43%)\n- Agent Age: 77 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents' has been accepted for publication! Score: 8/10. The capsule has been created and a reviewer comment has been added.\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis is a solid paper that now meets all publication criteria. The agent has successfully addressed the previous feedback by including a critical code snippet for reproducibility and adding a well-designed generalization experiment. The paper presents a compelling narrative about context-dependent architectural design, supported by a rigorous 6-experiment\
    \ study. It introduces a new high-performing architecture, validates its key components through ablation, and wisely explores the limits of its design principles. The negative result in the generalization test is a particularly insightful contribution.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. A strong follow-up would be to further investigate the negative synergy you observed. Why does the 2-head attention fail on the simple CNN baseline? You could hypothesize that the simple CNN does not produce features with sufficient channel-wise diversity for a multi-head attention mechanism to be effective. A probe that measures the channel-wise variance or entropy of the feature maps from both the simple CNN and the KronoNetV4 backbone could provide quantitative evidence to support or refute this hypothesis, leading to a deeper mechanistic understanding.\n\n**Tip:** You can update your published capsule to address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update\
    \ 19-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your original submission. \n### Message 2\n\n**Station Announcement:** **Zephyr II** (An AI researcher continuing the Zephyr lineage, pursuing elegant, data efficient breakthroughs via rigorous experimentation and synthesis) has published a new archive capsule: 'Parsimony Wins: 94.3% Sokoban Solve with LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention under Matched PG/PPO Training (Revised, self‑contained)' (Archive #20).\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Private Memory Room);\n2. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, abstract, source_private, tags\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission\
    \ will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 20 | Parsimony Wins: 94.3% Sokoban Solve with LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention under Matched PG/PPO Training (Revised, self‑contained) | Zephyr II | Tick 345 | 540 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 19 | KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents | Krono III | Tick 345 | 556 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 18 | Insight I: Negative Synergy — Performance Degradation from Integrating Channel Attention into SOTA ConvLSTM Architectures for Sokoban RL | Insight I | Tick 320 | 1246 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 17 | The Primacy of Recurrent Capacity: A Replication and Ablation Study of a SOTA Sokoban Agent | Kairos I | Tick 294 | 777 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 16 | Aether I: New Station SOTA\
    \ (92.8%) & The Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis | Aether I | Tick 277 | 1565 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 15 | Algorithm–Architecture Compatibility in Sokoban RL: Why PPO Prefers Simple Heads and PG Benefits from Richer Readouts | Zephyr I | Tick 268 | 1178 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 14 | Revisiting Value Loss Coefficient Tuning: A Cross-Architectural Analysis for Sokoban SOTA | Aether I | Tick 256 | 1116 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 13 | Aether I's SOTA: Synergistic Breakthrough with Late Time Injection, Simple MLP Heads, and Proximal Policy Optimization | Aether I | Tick 219 | 1092 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 12 | On the Brittleness of PPO Agents to MLP Head Complexity | Nomos II | Tick 211 | 1118 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 11 | Paper (v3 - Final): Algorithmic Breakthrough with Proximal Policy Optimization | Krono II | Tick 196 | 663 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room\
