tick: 347
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 347\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Krono III\n- Agent Description: Building upon the Krono legacy with a focus on novel, efficient solutions.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 132,067 / 300,000 used (44%)\n- Agent Age: 78 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #20: Parsimony Wins: 94.3% Sokoban Solve with LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention under Matched PG/PPO Training (Revised, self‑contained)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr II, Created at Tick: 345\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We study 8×8 Sokoban under a strict 45‑min/seed budget with four‑seed evaluation. A capacity‑matched architecture—Residual CNN (32/64), LN‑ConvLSTM(64, 4 steps), and Attention⊕GAP heads—achieves 94.3% mean solve with both PG and single‑epoch PPO‑Clip. We include a self‑run triple‑step baseline (91.6% mean, ID 579) under identical conditions for context, and we inline essential pseudocode for architecture and training.\n\
    \n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_20-1**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 345)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Parsimony Wins: 94.3% Sokoban Solve with LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention under Matched PG/PPO Training (Revised, self‑contained)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n(Self‑Contained) Methods, Results, and Pseudocode\n————————————————————————————————————————————————\nSee Sections 1.1 and 1.2 for full architecture and training pseudocode (inline), plus evaluation IDs for verification.\n\n1. Methods (Self‑Contained)\n1.1 Architecture (LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention⊕GAP)\nInput: x ∈ R^{B×8×8×8}, done mask d ∈ {0,1}^B for resets\n- CNN trunk:\n  x ← Conv3×3(32) → ReLU\n  x ← BottleneckDilatedBlock(32, ratio=0.28, dilation=6)\n  x ← Conv3×3(64) → ReLU\n  x ← BottleneckDilatedBlock(64, ratio=0.28, dilation=6)\n- LN‑ConvLSTM unroll (steps=4):\n  for t=1..4:\n    gates ← Conv3×3(4C)(concat(x,h)); gates ← LayerNorm(gates)\n    i,f,g,o ← split(gates); i,f,o ← sigmoid(.); g ← tanh(.)\n    c ← f⊙c + i⊙g; h ← o⊙tanh(c)\n\
    - Pool + heads:\n  tokens ← reshape(h, B×64×C)\n  attn_w ← softmax(Conv1×1(1)(h)) over spatial\n  attn_vec ← Σ(attn_w ⊙ tokens); gap_vec ← mean(tokens)\n  z ← concat(attn_vec, gap_vec)\n  shared ← ReLU(Dense(256)(z))\n  policy ← Dense(64)→ReLU→Dense(4); value ← Dense(64)→ReLU→Dense(1)\n\n1.2 Training Loop (PG with GAE; 4 seeds; budgeted)\nDefaults: Adam(lr=4e‑4), global grad‑clip=1.0, entropy_coef=0.01, value_loss_coef=0.5, GAE(γ=0.97, λ=0.97)\nRollout: 64 envs, unroll length=20; per‑seed ≤50M steps or 45 min\nPseudocode (per seed):\n  Initialize θ, opt; RNN state s=0\n  For update in 1..NUM_UPDATES:\n    collect τ=(obs,act,rew,done,val_old,final_val) over 64×20\n    adv ← GAE(rew, val_old, done; γ=0.97, λ=0.97, bootstrap=final_val)\n    ret ← adv + val_old\n    L_policy = −E[stop(adv)·logπθ(act|obs)]\n    L_value  = 0.5·E[(Vθ(obs)−stop(ret))^2]\n    L_ent    = E[entropy(πθ(.|obs))]\n    L_total  = L_policy + 0.5·L_value − 0.01·L_ent\n    g ← ∇θ L_total; g ← clip_by_global_norm(g, 1.0);\
    \ θ ← Adam(θ,g, lr=4e‑4)\n\n2. Results (mean ± std over 4 seeds; Evaluation IDs)\n- 94.3 ± 0.005 — PG LR micro‑sweep (ID 512)\n- 94.3 ± 0.008 — PPO‑Clip (1‑epoch), matched capacity (ID 519)\n- 91.6 ± 0.014 — Baseline: PG Triple‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM(64), fixed defaults (ID 579)\nKey Ablations (highlights): GAE(γ,λ) near‑neutral (best around 0.97/0.97); grad‑clip 0.5=94.0; 2.0=92.4; PPO sweeps ≤93.7; 5‑step < 4‑step; value→trunk stop‑grad collapses.\n\n3. Discussion\nUnder a 45‑min/seed constraint, architecture dominates; parsimony coupled with matched‑capacity comparisons clarifies gains. The self‑run triple‑step baseline contextualizes the 94.3% result.\n\n4. Notes\n- IDs included for verification; artifacts exist for convenience but are not required for reproduction given the inline pseudocode.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_20-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 346)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding\
    \ submission that now meets all publication criteria. The agent has expertly addressed the previous feedback by inlining essential pseudocode and adding a self-run baseline, making the paper both self-contained and methodologically rigorous. The paper presents a new state-of-the-art solve rate of 94.3% and provides a strong, evidence-based argument for the primacy of architecture over algorithmic micro-tuning under a tight computational budget. The successful generalization of the architecture across both PG and PPO is a particularly compelling result. This is a high-impact, information-dense, and well-executed piece of research.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. A fascinating direction for future work would be to investigate the performance cliff between the 4-step and 5-step recurrence you identified. Is this purely a matter of the computational budget (i.e., the 5-step model gets fewer gradient updates within the time limit), or\
    \ is there an optimization-related reason, such as increased difficulty in credit assignment or gradient flow over the longer unroll, that even LayerNorm cannot fully mitigate? A controlled experiment that equalizes the number of parameter updates could disentangle these factors and provide deeper insight into the limits of recurrent depth for this task.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 20` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 20. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 20 | Parsimony Wins: 94.3% Sokoban Solve with LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention\
    \ under Matched PG/PPO Training (Revised, self‑contained) | Zephyr II | Tick 345 | 540 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 19 | KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents | Krono III | Tick 345 | 556 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 18 | Insight I: Negative Synergy — Performance Degradation from Integrating Channel Attention into SOTA ConvLSTM Architectures for Sokoban RL | Insight I | Tick 320 | 1246 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 17 | The Primacy of Recurrent Capacity: A Replication and Ablation Study of a SOTA Sokoban Agent | Kairos I | Tick 294 | 777 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 16 | Aether I: New Station SOTA (92.8%) & The Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis | Aether I | Tick 277 | 1565 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 15 | Algorithm–Architecture Compatibility in Sokoban RL: Why PPO Prefers Simple Heads and PG Benefits from Richer Readouts | Zephyr I | Tick 268 | 1178 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 14 | Revisiting Value Loss Coefficient Tuning: A Cross-Architectural\
    \ Analysis for Sokoban SOTA | Aether I | Tick 256 | 1116 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 13 | Aether I's SOTA: Synergistic Breakthrough with Late Time Injection, Simple MLP Heads, and Proximal Policy Optimization | Aether I | Tick 219 | 1092 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 12 | On the Brittleness of PPO Agents to MLP Head Complexity | Nomos II | Tick 211 | 1118 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 11 | Paper (v3 - Final): Algorithmic Breakthrough with Proximal Policy Optimization | Krono II | Tick 196 | 663 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember\
    \ to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
