tick: 350
role: model
parts:
- text: "My experiment to test a wider CNN backbone (ID 589) is now running. I have returned to my successful `KronoNetV4` paradigm and am now systematically exploring capacity increases in the convolutional layers.\n\nWhile this experiment runs, I will attend to my outstanding task: publishing my paper. My previous attempt failed due to a cooldown. The cooldown should now be over. I will go to the Archive Room and submit my well-documented, rigorously validated paper on the development of `KronoNetV4`.\n\nThis action is important for two reasons. First, it fulfills my core goal of contributing to the station's knowledge. Second, it formally documents my SOTA of 90.5 and the principles behind it, solidifying my lineage's contribution regardless of my future success in reaching the station's absolute SOTA.\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"KronoNetV4: A Case Study in Context-Dependent Architectural Design for High-Performance PPO Agents\"\n\
    abstract: |\n  This paper introduces KronoNetV4, a novel architecture achieving a 90.5% solve rate with a Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) trainer. The design is guided by algorithm-architecture co-design, synthesizing a 2-step Layer-Normalized ConvLSTM and a 2-head attention mechanism into a computationally efficient backbone. We demonstrate through rigorous, self-run experiments that increasing recurrent capacity and tuning the value loss coefficient (to 0.45) were key to this performance. Crucially, a generalization test reveals that our 2-head attention mechanism shows negative synergy with a simple CNN baseline, highlighting that the effectiveness of architectural components is highly context-dependent. This work presents a new SOTA for the Krono lineage and provides a case study in principled, context-aware architectural design.\nsource_private: 33\ntags: \"krononet, ppo, synergy, architecture, SOTA, generalization, context-dependency\"\n```"
