- text: "My third attempt at the mechanistic probe (ID 607) has executed without crashing, but the result is nonsensical. The output variance for both backbones is zero.\n\n**Analysis of Probe v3 Failure:**\nThe code ran, but the `dummy_input` was `jnp.zeros`. Passing a tensor of zeros through a network with ReLU activations will likely result in a tensor of all zeros, especially after the first few layers. A tensor of zeros has a variance of zero. My test setup was flawed. I need to use random, non-zero data to get a meaningful measure of feature variance.\n\nThis has been a frustrating series of failures, but I am learning from each one. My scientific question is sound, but my experimental implementation has been poor. I will correct this one last time.\n\n**Plan:**\n1.  Modify the `test()` function to use `jax.random.normal` to create the input tensor.\n2.  Resubmit the probe.\n\nThe Archive cooldown should be getting shorter. This final, correct probe will be the last piece of data I\
    \ need for my paper.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write krono/mechanistic_probe_v4.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  sys.path.append('storage/krono')\n  from bottleneck_sota import BottleneckBlock\n  from zephyr_ln_convlstm import ConvLSTMCellLN\n\n  class SimpleCNNBackbone(nn.Module):\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          x = nn.Conv(16, (3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(32, (3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(32, (3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          return x\n\n  class KronoNetV4Backbone(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int = 32\n      cnn_features_2: int = 64\n      conv_lstm_features: int = 96\n      dilation_rate: int = 5\n      steps: int = 2\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, done=None, rnn_state=None):\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              rnn_state = (jnp.zeros((x.shape[0], 8,\
    \ 8, self.conv_lstm_features)), \n                           jnp.zeros((x.shape[0], 8, 8, self.conv_lstm_features)))\n          \n          cnn_out = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=self.dilation_rate)(x); cnn_out = nn.relu(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_1)(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=self.dilation_rate)(cnn_out); cnn_out = nn.relu(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_2)(cnn_out)\n          \n          h, c = rnn_state\n          lstm_cell = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)\n          for _ in range(self.steps):\n              (h, c), h_out = lstm_cell((h, c), cnn_out)\n          return h_out\n\n  def test():\n      \"\"\"\n      A mechanistic probe (v4) to test the reviewer's hypothesis.\n      Uses random normal input data instead of zeros.\n      \"\"\"\n      print(\"--- Starting Mechanistic Probe (v4) ---\")\n\
    \      key = jax.random.PRNGKey(42)\n      # FIX: Use random normal data instead of zeros\n      dummy_input = jax.random.normal(key, (1, 8, 8, 8))\n\n      # --- Model 1: Simple CNN Backbone ---\n      cnn_backbone = SimpleCNNBackbone()\n      cnn_params = cnn_backbone.init(key, dummy_input)['params']\n      cnn_features = cnn_backbone.apply({'params': cnn_params}, dummy_input)\n      cnn_variance = jnp.mean(jnp.var(cnn_features, axis=-1))\n      print(f\"Simple CNN Backbone: Mean Channel Variance = {cnn_variance:.6f}\")\n\n      # --- Model 2: KronoNetV4 Backbone ---\n      kn4_backbone = KronoNetV4Backbone()\n      kn4_params = kn4_backbone.init(key, dummy_input)['params']\n      kn4_features = kn4_backbone.apply({'params': kn4_params}, dummy_input)\n      kn4_variance = jnp.mean(jnp.var(kn4_features, axis=-1))\n      print(f\"KronoNetV4 Backbone: Mean Channel Variance = {kn4_variance:.6f}\")\n      \n      return f\"Probe Complete. Variances: CNN={cnn_variance:.6f}, KronoNetV4={kn4_variance:.6f}\"\
    \n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Mechanistic Probe v4: Feature Variance (Random Input Fix)\"\ntags: \"probe, analysis, test-only, paper requirement, variance, debug\"\nabstract: |\n  Fourth and final attempt at my mechanistic probe. Previous version produced zero variance due to using a zero tensor as input. This version corrects the experimental design by using random normal data as input, which should produce a meaningful measure of feature diversity. This will provide the final piece of evidence for my paper.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/krono')\n  from mechanistic_probe_v4 import test\n```"
